            "annotations": [],
            "logprobs": [],
            "text": "{\"strengths\":[\"Accurately identifies the external cost imposed on nearby residents and distinguishes private from social costs.\",\"Develops the full overproduction mechanism: MSC exceeds MPC, market output is Qm, and the socially efficient output Qs is lower.\",\"Clearly explains welfare loss and overallocation of resources, while correctly noting that pollution does not imply all production is socially undesirable.\"],\"improvements\":[\"Add the required negative-production-externality diagram: price/cost/benefit on the vertical axis, quantity on the horizontal axis, MSC above MPC, MPB = MSB, Qm to the right of Qs, and the welfare-loss area over excess output.\"],\"mistakes\":[\"Missing required diagram\"],\"examinerComment\":\"Estimated practice mark: 8/10. This is a precise and well-developed written explanation of market failure from a negative externality of production. The missing diagram is the material limitation because it was necessary for this task; a correctly labelled diagram would visibly support the cost curves, excess output and welfare loss explained in the prose.\",\"studyNext\":\"Practise drawing the negative-production-externality model from memory, then annotate Qm, Qs and the welfare-loss area and link each feature to one sentence of explanation.\",\"assessmentFormat\":\"paper_1_a\",\"paper\":\"paper_1\",\"questionPart\":\"a\",\"levelRelevance\":\"shared_sl_hl\",\"assessmentSkills\":[\"economic_analysis\",\"diagram_explanation\",\"application\"],\"commandTerm\":\"explain\",\"commandTermLabel\":\"Explain\",\"syllabusUnit\":\"unit_2\",\"syllabusTopic\":\"2.8\",\"topicLabel\":\"Externalities and common pool/common access resources\",\"classificationConfidence\":\"high\",\"markingConfidence\":\"high\",\"diagramExpected\":true,\"diagramSubmitted\":false,\"diagramAssessmentStatus\":\"not_submitted\",\"workingsExpected\":false,\"workingsSubmitted\":false,\"workingsAssessmentStatus\":\"not_relevant\",\"attachmentContent\":\"none\",\"assessableEarned\":8,\"markBreakdown\":[{\"label\":\"Knowledge and terminology\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"Uses market failure, external costs, marginal social cost, marginal private cost, MPB, MSB and welfare loss accurately.\"},{\"label\":\"Economic analysis\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"Explains the causal chain from unpriced third-party health costs to MSC > MPC, overproduction and reduced social surplus clearly and accurately.\"},{\"label\":\"Application to context\",\"awarded\":3,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"The cement-dust example is relevant and is used coherently to illustrate third-party health costs, although outside application was not essential to this task.\"},{\"label\":\"Diagram\",\"awarded\":0,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"No submitted diagram shows MSC above MPC, Qm and Qs, or the welfare-loss area required to support this externality mechanism.\"}],\"bandRationale\":\"The answer securely fits the 7–8 band: it is focused, accurate and well-developed in writing, with a complete negative-production-externality mechanism. It cannot move higher because the necessary diagram is absent, leaving an important element of the task unmet.\",\"limitations\":[\"No diagram was provided to show the negative production externality, market and socially efficient outputs, and welfare loss.\"],\"examinerJudgment\":{\"task\":\"Explain how pollution from production can cause market failure.\",\"demonstrated\":\"A complete written explanation using a cement-pollution example: external costs mean MSC exceeds MPC, market output Qm exceeds efficient output Qs, and excess output creates welfare loss and resource overallocation.\",\"materialLimitations\":[\"The necessary negative-production-externality diagram is absent.\"],\"selectedBand\":\"7-8\",\"withinBand\":\"upper\",\"diagramEffect\":\"missing_material\",\"summary\":\"Strong, accurate written analysis, materially limited by omission of the required supporting diagram.\"},\"componentEvaluation\":null}"
